  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nFour plasma cell clusters (7–10) were identified from a pan-cancer scRNA-seq atlas of tumor-infiltrating B/plasma cells.  Cluster assignment used canonical plasma markers (e.g., CD38, JCHAIN, SDC1) and differential expression analysis (avg_log2FC, pct.1 vs pct.2).  No batch or tumor-type biases were noted.  Key observations in the marker tables:  \n• Cluster 7 shows high immunoglobulin light chains plus ER/chaperone genes.  \n• Cluster 8 is dominated by heat-shock and immediate-early stress genes.  \n• Cluster 9 is enriched for class-switched heavy chains (IgA/IgG) and JCHAIN.  \n• Cluster 10 is defined by metallothioneins and metal-homeostasis factors.\n\nKeywords  \n1. ER stress / UPR  \n2. Heat-shock proteins  \n3. Class switching (IgA/IgG)  \n4. Metallothioneins  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nMZB1, XBP1, HSPA6, MT2A  \n\nFunctional Phenotype Assignments  \n\nCluster 7: “High-Output Secretory Plasma Cells”  \nMarkers: IGLV6-57, IGKV4-1, IGKC, IGHA1, MZB1, DERL3, FKBP11, HSP90B1, SSR4  \nRationale: Strong upregulation of immunoglobulin chains plus ER-resident chaperones (MZB1, HSP90B1, DERL3, FKBP11) and SEC pathway components (SSR4) indicates an expanded secretory apparatus for antibody assembly and secretion.\n\nCluster 8: “Stress-Adaptive Plasma Cells”  \nMarkers: HSPA6, HSPA1A/B, HSPB1, DNAJB1, JUN, FOS, ATF3, XBP1  \nRationale: Dominant heat-shock proteins (HSP family), unfolded protein response TF XBP1, and immediate-early genes (JUN, FOS, ATF3) reflect a stress-responsive state. These cells likely adapt to proteotoxic stress while maintaining secretory function.\n\nCluster 9: “Class-Switched Effector Plasma Cells”  \nMarkers: IGHA2, IGHG1/2, IGKC, JCHAIN, TNFRSF17  \nRationale: Enrichment for switched heavy-chain isotypes (IgA, IgG subclasses), JCHAIN, and the BCMA receptor (TNFRSF17) typifies mature effector plasma cells secreting class-switched antibodies.\n\nCluster 10: “Metallothionein-Rich Regulatory Plasma Cells”  \nMarkers: MT2A, MT1X, MT1E, MT1F, LGALS3, S100A11  \nRationale: High metallothionein expression (MT family) indicates roles in metal ion buffering and redox regulation. Co-upregulation of LGALS3 (galectin-3) and S100A11 suggests potential immunomodulatory or tissue-repair functions.\n\nOverall Discussion  \nThe four plasma cell clusters segregate by their dominant functional modules: cluster 7 by secretory machinery, cluster 8 by stress adaptation, cluster 9 by effector antibody isotypes, and cluster 10 by metal-homeostasis/regulatory programs.  This stratification likely reflects different microenvironmental demands (e.g., oxidative stress, antigen specificity) within tumors.  These phenotypes may differentially influence antitumor immunity, therapeutic response, or immune escape.\n\nNext Steps & Clarifications  \n1. Validate secretory capacity (cluster 7) via quantitative Ig secretion assays.  \n2. Test stress-resilience (cluster 8) under induced ER stress (e.g., tunicamycin).  \n3. Profile antigen specificity of class-switched IgA/G (cluster 9) by single-cell BCR sequencing.  \n4. Examine metal-ion binding and immunoregulatory cytokine profiles in cluster 10.  \n5. Assess spatial localization of each plasma cell subtype within tumor niches.",
